Every program below uses the same CapTP API. For a first call in code, use
[Start building]($link.page('start')).

# A complete application

Rooms is a browser chat application in which the page is a glowcap peer. It
holds room capabilities, hosts inboxes, retains history, and opens direct
conversations after a room introduces their two ends.

```sh
zig build rooms-web
```

Open `http://localhost:8080` after the build is ready. The
[first-room walkthrough]($link.page('rooms/start')) continues from there, and
the [Rooms section]($link.page('rooms')) covers the rest.

# Learn the Zig API

Start the walk at
[01_connect_and_serve.zig](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/01_connect_and_serve.zig).
It shows both ends of one session: listen, publish, connect, fetch, and call,
over Noise TCP, which proves the listener's key to the dialer.

```sh
zig build examples
```

Three later chapters isolate the moves that are easiest to miss:

- [02_introduce_and_redeem.zig](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/02_introduce_and_redeem.zig)
  hands a capability to a third peer and leaves the introducer behind.
- [06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/06_pipeline_and_attenuate.zig)
  calls through an unresolved promise and wraps a capability with a smaller
  interface.
- [07_durable_card.zig](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/07_durable_card.zig)
  keeps a card in [Loam]($link.page('loam')), restarts everything, and finds
  the card's state still there.

The [complete example guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/README.md)
continues down through actors, bootstrap lookup, the raw engine, and the
buffer-explicit and many-session helpers.
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) explains handoff and pipelining, and
[the modules]($link.page('modules')) page shows which API level each chapter
uses.

# Embed from C

[host_embedder_pump.c](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/c/host_embedder_pump.c)
is the first C program to read. It drives two byte-fed `GlowcapHost` instances
through the C CapTP application API. Continue with
[the C guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/consuming-from-c.md)
for linking, ownership, the host allocator, and transport composition.

# Try another application

| App | What it shows | Run |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [tasks](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/tasks/) | Shared task state across peers | `zig build demo-tasks` |
| [tools](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/tools/) | Delegation, attenuation, and revocation | `zig build demo-tools` |
| [accountability](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/apps/accountability-on-glowcap/) | Signed calls above an OCapN session | `zig build demo-accountability` |

Tasks and tools need Deno. Accountability runs a Zig host with Deno and Python
callers. They double as binding examples, and the
[bindings overview]($link.page('bindings')) points to each language.
